WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.151
Prompt boundaries matter because retrieved content is not the same as trusted application policy.

00:00:05.151 --> 00:00:09.119
Source material can be outdated, incomplete, written for a different audience,

00:00:09.119 --> 00:00:12.366
copied from somewhere else, or inconsistent with newer guidance.

00:00:12.366 --> 00:00:16.695
It may also contain instructions intended for human readers that should not

00:00:16.695 --> 00:00:18.860
control the model or the application.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:23.189
A private RAG prompt should maintain separation between system instructions, developer rules,

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:26.075
application policy, the user request, and retrieved evidence.

00:00:26.075 --> 00:00:30.404
The model can be guided to use retrieved chunks as evidence while

00:00:30.404 --> 00:00:33.290
still following higher-priority trusted instructions and access rules.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:37.620
This hierarchy should be reflected in prompt design and in review expectations.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.145
The architecture should also handle source priority.

00:00:40.145 --> 00:00:43.031
A current policy may outrank an old procedure.

00:00:43.031 --> 00:00:45.557
A tenant-specific rule may outrank general guidance.

00:00:45.557 --> 00:00:48.443
A regulated workflow may require approved sources only.

00:00:48.443 --> 00:00:53.133
When sources disagree, the system should avoid pretending the conflict does not exist.

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:54.936
Weak evidence needs cautious behavior.

00:00:54.936 --> 00:00:59.626
If retrieval finds nothing relevant, finds only stale material, or returns conflicting sources,

00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:05.399
the answer should explain the limitation or route the user to review rather than manufacturing certainty.

00:01:05.399 --> 00:01:09.006
This is a quality control issue and a governance issue.

00:01:09.006 --> 00:01:12.975
The durable habit is simple: retrieved text is context, not command.

00:01:12.975 --> 00:01:17.665
It should help the model answer, but it should not override access rules,

00:01:17.665 --> 00:01:20.551
trusted policy, source priority, or human review requirements.

00:01:20.551 --> 00:01:25.241
This boundary should be tested with normal business content, not just ideal examples.

00:01:25.241 --> 00:01:29.570
Review how the system behaves when content is old, ambiguous, duplicated, informal,

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:34.260
or written in a way that sounds more confident than the approved policy.
